#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499498 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f6b9f6c-95f0-5c70-b22d-f60c0e9d20da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Unit Interval for a Root of an Integer"}] :question/id #uuid "83d31d9c-626c-5711-b7cf-1417f78d3347" :question/math-academy-id "e-4892" :question/problem "Between which two consecutive whole numbers does ![](images/a8/a8298b42ebec3ef477881da10ec20db7e50de91740a8a950f104e6be8603bb82.svg) lie?" :question/worked-solution "To find which two consecutive whole numbers ![](images/a8/a8298b42ebec3ef477881da10ec20db7e50de91740a8a950f104e6be8603bb82.svg) lies between, we can compare ![](images/75/75729a0d755ed343ab24a2288c1c0edc765a2cffd3a70e982daf9b2b68785969.svg) to nearby perfect cubes.\n\n- The closest perfect cube less than ![](images/75/75729a0d755ed343ab24a2288c1c0edc765a2cffd3a70e982daf9b2b68785969.svg) is ![](images/67/67b1b7ef88f956cc914bdc85cacbd83df6a22c511224e85d3d9aeeb80eee9392.svg) since ![](images/e9/e9c62ca9ae68e481505f45bff035d951215eb0beea7f3289b7b9f550bdc99560.svg)\n\n- The closest perfect cube greater than ![](images/75/75729a0d755ed343ab24a2288c1c0edc765a2cffd3a70e982daf9b2b68785969.svg) is ![](images/3b/3b0ce4686cd13ee54ad467f989a69a337a7037b78fd2a14d562a1d299ba1e4b5.svg) since ![](images/4b/4b7e4a25efbec891e74d07e60fc6b93b6cb3865b9384e0f388bae2ec19be359d.svg)\n\nThis means ![](images/75/75729a0d755ed343ab24a2288c1c0edc765a2cffd3a70e982daf9b2b68785969.svg) is between ![](images/c6/c64ba31f35ed2d1f23f3827854effc6461b82c7cfa7f27694bf545acb565c90a.svg) and ![](images/54/54f588e00853e81c9aececc72d9474b6816aaec1c979ca3d940c0030c2e401ab.svg)\n![](images/eb/eb5e875b9bf93096c7a1745a02fae75a8be75ab827b1f624fab5e2804cdedb73.svg)\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives\n![](images/9d/9d2b62267e10537690522252c069b2a24b1e95b92321ddad314ada26aa0114b6.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/a8/a8298b42ebec3ef477881da10ec20db7e50de91740a8a950f104e6be8603bb82.svg) lies between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/85/85ab4d14e4ae710116ac84de4b03b3cf35723ab969b7abfba97889c497974147.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499496 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83d6ac5f-99e4-5420-9047-1c43a50b43a4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Root of an Integer"}] :question/id #uuid "80a9441b-918f-5c2e-9da2-6d8d0312d00d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4894" :question/problem "Estimate the value of ![](images/3e/3e196c5b200d5538850094f358f3c55d2631e745e813769d3be43035a9e866db.svg) rounded to ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) decimal place." :question/worked-solution "To estimate ![](images/3e/3e196c5b200d5538850094f358f3c55d2631e745e813769d3be43035a9e866db.svg) we first find the two perfect squares closest to ![](images/1a/1a4409994d80d42e52672c89d85bb2a11c557d555a38ad6d614b78bd78330b9b.svg)\n\nThe closest perfect squares are ![](images/82/8250f3d333487008c9f8ff6f21c2fcc3f67cc8b061380b5e4457a5f9a7353945.svg) and ![](images/b8/b8b8cb2562d9c82f9c03088fc18d14d7750334c69cf803b535d79b1c73986b77.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/30/30ecc456cab2448c058eb3e738bdb4da96c41886e3a4ca451278ebf3792544a1.svg) we know that the square root of ![](images/cb/cbc9c7a84ec901d819d33b39b58420fe3e7a6dbba88800a66fcfdc4da472ac78.svg) lies between ![](images/37/37e17c4a2302e6352fb7186621c9c43bb5e99d43a8a0bc700c0181d36c6cc915.svg) and ![](images/6a/6a9cf85ae3a9cce8f2443a234fb1a16c612d847a446261ea740d93d5f1c468cf.svg)\n\n![](images/f9/f9d2536ecb1a4d163997e6415b21ff95094074c7fb521a535fc41d33bb0f0173.png)\n\nSince ![](images/cb/cbc9c7a84ec901d819d33b39b58420fe3e7a6dbba88800a66fcfdc4da472ac78.svg) lies between ![](images/16/1617868c0d9d78c4948ad3bc749cfd748e90ec411c8b5260787cd9e3a2b663f3.svg) and ![](images/63/63b1758c5ce5a0b6741c40d16d53081d254a842379c3663cc6961d8a8b599904.svg) we expect ![](images/36/36c2915c61e115b1d5c8f055defd37ee7e73858145e203088a49d5a0d79a6e23.svg) to lie between ![](images/37/37e17c4a2302e6352fb7186621c9c43bb5e99d43a8a0bc700c0181d36c6cc915.svg) and ![](images/6a/6a9cf85ae3a9cce8f2443a234fb1a16c612d847a446261ea740d93d5f1c468cf.svg)\n\nLet's test decimals near the middle by squaring them:\n![](images/68/684b3544dbe006f99281e2a9f0b5b780002ed587b7de58d83c7ff81a1b33b148.svg)\n\nWe can see that ![](images/cb/cbc9c7a84ec901d819d33b39b58420fe3e7a6dbba88800a66fcfdc4da472ac78.svg) lies between ![](images/c4/c4a4623be7f3d5660215b04af294514485d84bef208d5097f723feb72ec328da.svg) and ![](images/83/83ba2899aad41f580bf181cc53d40d2a1fa4fb881acd86fb09324b9dd4027550.svg) so ![](images/de/de19f2fd6bae35a5893d4ac5e7704c12750044637eb4750842141f5b1d0b15a9.svg)\n\nNext, we determine which value is closer to ![](images/95/9591360f4ce469dc90934057a5ea17d0eaaa61ba52a1bb515a21d0a41fcdb966.svg)\n\n- The distance from ![](images/cb/cbc9c7a84ec901d819d33b39b58420fe3e7a6dbba88800a66fcfdc4da472ac78.svg) to ![](images/aa/aad398006b3c5b9cf716a7455bc6aa9f7abc68b57a5de763efb7c7f5928a9547.svg) is ![](images/61/61d92f48929a5d8d900087d1765a4b82640a5f93fbe9124879e0ba5fa4954a24.svg)\n\n- The distance from ![](images/cb/cbc9c7a84ec901d819d33b39b58420fe3e7a6dbba88800a66fcfdc4da472ac78.svg) to ![](images/92/92ab44e79c1791a780944b6449d9a8af8c43fbd4e7323bf7f995dca1fc5f45da.svg) is ![](images/51/51197b9d01e2d0c848a9f8728f00a6dc518521d749848d5a4d2816f1d8e06aa3.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/cb/cbc9c7a84ec901d819d33b39b58420fe3e7a6dbba88800a66fcfdc4da472ac78.svg) is much closer to ![](images/3a/3a199a75daabe803a6adb7279c9b2b4022a0c2989e41b5611b478e2d8322c4f9.svg) ![](images/36/36c2915c61e115b1d5c8f055defd37ee7e73858145e203088a49d5a0d79a6e23.svg) is closer to ![](images/bf/bff510d56c2a8598a777cec76166de972132e5d73f2ef73dcb2983b00bdac1e9.svg)\n\nTherefore, rounded to ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) decimal place, ![](images/b7/b7f0378eb41d1ca9f785366f04d5b1b11a9632cd40d694140c235f0033ab0bcb.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499463 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77b54737-402a-5ce2-acda-ea818dd80b33" :knowledge-point/title "Ordering a Set of Irrational Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "40e25a07-249d-5649-a737-532e2634f702" :question/math-academy-id "e-51599" :question/problem "Which of the following irrational numbers is the greatest?\n\n![](images/db/db06ddd333f1212337e37a5090aa50607f35c210768614b2c76becb267a438b5.svg) ![](images/70/70735c1de4ef1fd1c52dc25425367780dacb19dd268c9d21c774a15cb11fe5b5.svg) ![](images/5a/5ace00a4ef554b533736ae8bc220bd62c9f320417994f9f8eb02f41ad7cee1b0.svg) ![](images/e3/e39ea2a02df28854d7ff2da05e7250cb556fe78b88b836eb29fa4a0ff869714e.svg) ![](images/ba/ba20e4e917e21ab13b9aab507e09632255fccd6346f08f31333fffd468f3eea1.svg)" :question/worked-solution "To identify the greatest irrational number, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each root lies.\n\n- ![](images/c9/c983181e93167656339df404a2031696e0409b558cf70676ed574e4367cf5ed6.svg) lies between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/b1/b1288d1c08b90d72fcd319980da7943ea4941b9f259ece4086d8b24b1bc0cfb2.svg) since ![](images/19/193c32af60f9d54dc14b9273e47f9ff3ebe2ee5739ff1e8c1d88ea8425a57836.svg) and ![](images/90/908c8ab7da1593180589b78db1d4fd59c993575ee517ef304f9b41a2d9ab975e.svg)\n\n- ![](images/ba/ba1ca097eda9cfed0062b1845069809f38d1a6a67e0b2adcea778090d5d4bb36.svg) lies between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/b1/b1288d1c08b90d72fcd319980da7943ea4941b9f259ece4086d8b24b1bc0cfb2.svg) since ![](images/19/193c32af60f9d54dc14b9273e47f9ff3ebe2ee5739ff1e8c1d88ea8425a57836.svg) and ![](images/90/908c8ab7da1593180589b78db1d4fd59c993575ee517ef304f9b41a2d9ab975e.svg)\n\n- ![](images/39/398d865ecee9dba51e8ba4918a187f483b9291ff6ae0d2032be0d95d40190ff7.svg) lies between ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) and ![](images/39/39c9d5c6b02f72e65180a1a782af0404d25977c3627c5778e1bcb676286506c4.svg) since ![](images/7a/7adac413db5d45050f3eb7abbd3482761301903db41f2a418d142db2f44b64a0.svg) and ![](images/e9/e9c62ca9ae68e481505f45bff035d951215eb0beea7f3289b7b9f550bdc99560.svg)\n\n- ![](images/d2/d26797521f0c40831b132a3be0b136dcf886987344fc6ab289c6640a75c512b2.svg) lies between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/e9/e94ce8a42ab6b97ee9c979dcc0a002b557e1c6339e5950f601a66a9af91f67e5.svg) since ![](images/0a/0a53cb6ef00f2cee34191c318c35c03772be8c31188a3d08a3934c0bd7bcecc1.svg) and ![](images/4b/4b7e4a25efbec891e74d07e60fc6b93b6cb3865b9384e0f388bae2ec19be359d.svg)\n\n- ![](images/ba/ba20e4e917e21ab13b9aab507e09632255fccd6346f08f31333fffd468f3eea1.svg) lies between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/e9/e94ce8a42ab6b97ee9c979dcc0a002b557e1c6339e5950f601a66a9af91f67e5.svg) since ![](images/7c/7cd932842d9ae0d5fc425ee041b38155430ef8cca572adf3c9e4e4d2983a617e.svg) and ![](images/42/42e829426bf6a9f4c1a5afda3bf7d39f1cd3fa3c58b05aa37a2103e440081a32.svg)\n\nComparing these bounds, the greatest number must be between ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) and ![](images/fe/fe34f715005da1a8b682c9650a76785673fdf25c3b7c7546bb94824470c54caf.svg) So, it is either ![](images/c9/c983181e93167656339df404a2031696e0409b558cf70676ed574e4367cf5ed6.svg) or ![](images/d8/d86b01270851dbe9d7eae65f51266ecad5b7361429ee5d131acb5f0e9e58cd5e.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/b9/b9545d45e50201e2e348eea7a79a0e8a480765e53c75cc619306f71569413a32.svg) we know that ![](images/a0/a06ddf203ca30569ef240823ab98f890e6418e528eb5b4a1e87074b52c500d0b.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/ba/ba1ca097eda9cfed0062b1845069809f38d1a6a67e0b2adcea778090d5d4bb36.svg) is the greatest number among the given options."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e25ce9f-c93f-5dc0-9e81-2093663c6202" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decimal Interval for a Root of an Integer"}] :question/id #uuid "809623d1-30aa-5672-ac4f-f3c539a51eb7" :question/math-academy-id "e-51600" :question/problem "Between which two consecutive tenths does ![](images/4b/4be23abb7ad197619ea40d11d47cf1fdbb147bfdb686ebb3ec3edf35fe86a11a.svg) lie?" :question/worked-solution "To find the two consecutive tenths between which ![](images/4b/4be23abb7ad197619ea40d11d47cf1fdbb147bfdb686ebb3ec3edf35fe86a11a.svg) lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket ![](images/c4/c470a153cbef9a736d6098e83a3cfe29bd8aab79763d40e7d037249050243a38.svg).\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers ![](images/4b/4be23abb7ad197619ea40d11d47cf1fdbb147bfdb686ebb3ec3edf35fe86a11a.svg) lies.\nWe know that ![](images/0a/0a53cb6ef00f2cee34191c318c35c03772be8c31188a3d08a3934c0bd7bcecc1.svg) and ![](images/99/99407afccad5e3e8923e1f7baefd9c2c39a0527a7891c4fbeed2f278831675b3.svg).\nSince ![](images/52/5283496580d408c3985a17e58b695eff54043bfcaaee8f2289f795ee2bb3383e.svg), we know that ![](images/88/88c76039d22e912b07971b62a9f1625d144396596eb04a37713b2b8d5145a9a8.svg).\n\nNext, we can test decimals between ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) and ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) to narrow down the interval:\n![](images/73/732e30063191974603e50ade6b2d95bbe7064d4a29d1a03f607f73b65545e3e4.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/7e/7ed21dc216d8118e893b07314d80a792d90411459ee9ea6f57de2ddbdb9a2f60.svg), we can write:\n![](images/79/79e2114a0d701bc50c5b8296a1379cc1e86348678c137f32b59b16f3ad40530a.svg)\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n![](images/c6/c6f904b992c68db63b37b809d99a8f385c55c1218d7eae5dbcf430698010e4e0.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/4b/4be23abb7ad197619ea40d11d47cf1fdbb147bfdb686ebb3ec3edf35fe86a11a.svg) lies between ![](images/a5/a5d561e3e5649657cfd9407bba6b91bee82f1a2c0d2d745e9c168b2cdf060ed6.svg) and ![](images/96/96fb4a6bbc8edb4f40fafdecd98c0c3a20d38e9449a3cba5170ff6b1f7371537.svg)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f6b9f6c-95f0-5c70-b22d-f60c0e9d20da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Unit Interval for a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9977f504-cb84-5649-879a-0432332558d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9977f504-cb84-5649-879a-0432332558d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499057} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a425152-f79b-5599-82e1-b1e6ff1bffe0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499382} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "348c7168-4c39-55ae-a155-ffab719b5de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "348c7168-4c39-55ae-a155-ffab719b5de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498848} :answer-field/id #uuid "b534bb7e-9b84-5245-a11d-21bce84b0169" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0afb67b2-5531-5190-bc7f-fa3f3f29c54e" :question/math-academy-id "q-555450" :question/problem "The value of $\\sqrt{57}$ lies between two consecutive whole numbers $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt{57}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect squares that are just below and just above $57.$\n\nThe perfect squares closest to $57$ are $49$ and $64.$ We have\n\n$$\n49<57<64.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{49} & <\\sqrt{57}<\\sqrt{64} \\\\ 7 & <\\sqrt{57}<8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2b5ce9c24d223674d04686a577347e43cd40728194596e75df7c0faa006d2888.png)\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt{57}$ lies between the consecutive whole numbers $7$ and $8.$ \n\nTherefore, since $a<b,$ we have $a=7$ and $b=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f6b9f6c-95f0-5c70-b22d-f60c0e9d20da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Unit Interval for a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a5405a3-f024-5ca7-9c31-e52bdd90919e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498860} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5be66a-4987-509f-a5c7-96305e7b6c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$19$ and $21$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498941} {:answer/id #uuid "906fde7a-2155-5f3f-bae3-b4e898a2fbe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ and $21$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499045} {:answer/id #uuid "d483e280-210e-55d0-ac9d-b281cdb9850a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499204} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d8194e-c67d-5470-826e-4c119b115fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a5405a3-f024-5ca7-9c31-e52bdd90919e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498860} :answer-field/id #uuid "43fe2956-91ea-5f92-88da-967ba3ef54af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f73f19a-494d-5194-ba7a-fdcda5923013" :question/math-academy-id "q-555452" :question/problem "Between which two consecutive whole numbers does $\\sqrt{40}$ lie?" :question/worked-solution "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt{40}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect squares that are just below and just above $40.$\n\nThe perfect squares closest to $40$ are $36$ and $49.$ We have\n\n$$\n36<40<49.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{36} & <\\sqrt{40}<\\sqrt{49} \\\\ 6 & <\\sqrt{40}<7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/5c/5c2775762ff274451dfd3258bacbede7240e6bad25b782cc86a76484e61d31bb.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{40}$ lies between $6$ and $7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77b54737-402a-5ce2-acda-ea818dd80b33" :knowledge-point/title "Ordering a Set of Irrational Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f0faf7-bc3b-5987-b926-60ef170179b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498771} {:answer/id #uuid "222f9576-892b-5013-8880-e641d57b3eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498806} {:answer/id #uuid "3a95a1f3-57cb-53e9-a86e-eb6ce17f0bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{90}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498863} {:answer/id #uuid "a05d4d7e-5c15-5e06-83d0-1d348de7fb94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499072} {:answer/id #uuid "cc020065-3a08-59fa-9688-70e25b9deae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a05d4d7e-5c15-5e06-83d0-1d348de7fb94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499072} :answer-field/id #uuid "d47f6963-cb9f-513f-a758-f012e520f227" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c82eb124-d241-562e-a9aa-60dccb777294" :question/math-academy-id "q-555455" :question/problem "Which of the following irrational numbers is the greatest?" :question/worked-solution "To identify the greatest irrational number, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each root lies.\n\n- $\\sqrt{29}$ lies between $5$ and $6,$ since ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{27}$ lies between $5$ and $6,$ since ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{40}$ lies between $3$ and $4,$ since ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${4}^{3}=64.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{90}$ lies between $4$ and $5,$ since ${4}^{3}=64$ and ${5}^{3}=125.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{20}$ lies between $4$ and $5,$ since ${4}^{2}=16$ and ${5}^{2}=25.$\n\nComparing these bounds, the greatest number must be between $5$ and $6.$ So, it is either $\\sqrt{29}$ or $\\sqrt{27}.$ \n\nSince $29>27,$ we know that $\\sqrt{29}>\\sqrt{27}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{29}$ is the greatest number among the given options."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e25ce9f-c93f-5dc0-9e81-2093663c6202" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decimal Interval for a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "243c9113-299e-59cf-a357-0cefe6c4d08b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.4$ and $3.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498810} {:answer/id #uuid "34eef6b1-dd77-5a22-acd0-de325f2fbc1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.7$ and $6.8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498851} {:answer/id #uuid "cf76145a-7d25-5f2d-aedb-75bbb68c9d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.6$ and $2.7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499189} {:answer/id #uuid "d71b384f-4240-578d-a5d5-a91316c0ae60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.6$ and $3.7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499212} {:answer/id #uuid "ee937844-8f4e-5825-a105-dc5e64b79133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.5$ and $3.6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee937844-8f4e-5825-a105-dc5e64b79133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.5$ and $3.6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499266} :answer-field/id #uuid "c01a9710-0c3b-5f44-83dc-6039b3f885a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92983ecf-db5c-5c74-b988-756fcd6337e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-555461" :question/problem "Between which two consecutive tenths does $\\sqrt[3]{45}$ lie?" :question/worked-solution "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{45}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $45$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{45}$ lies.\nWe know that ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${4}^{3}=64$.\nSince $27<45<64$, we know that $3<\\sqrt[3]{45}<4$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $3$ and $4$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3.4}^{3} & =39.304 \\\\ {3.5}^{3} & =42.875 \\\\ {3.6}^{3} & =46.656\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $42.875<45<46.656$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{3.5}^{3}<45<{3.6}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n3.5<\\sqrt[3]{45}<3.6\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{45}$ lies between $3.5$ and $3.6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83d6ac5f-99e4-5420-9047-1c43a50b43a4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c53433-b0f7-519f-a4a8-b7ca0a577929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498741} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7ddc50-b46c-5c7d-8cb0-1357f2ea142b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498903} {:answer/id #uuid "b299ca2b-d607-5531-8e61-3649f65397c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499129} {:answer/id #uuid "b803fef8-bfa3-5bca-ab56-a503730f1578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499141} {:answer/id #uuid "e09a3792-a321-5f02-bd73-1d384a4b4fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a7ddc50-b46c-5c7d-8cb0-1357f2ea142b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498903} :answer-field/id #uuid "526aa133-91ed-5e0d-b820-92a175d01133" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b73b2161-1f16-50bc-aeef-165cf5e2f96c" :question/math-academy-id "q-555464" :question/problem "Which of the following is the best estimate for $\\sqrt{55}?$" :question/worked-solution "To estimate $\\sqrt{55},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $55.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $49={7}^{2}$ and $64={8}^{2}.$\n\nSince $49<55<64,$ we know that the square root of $55$ lies between $7$ and $8.$ This tells us that $6.8$ and $27.5$ are incorrect estimates.\n\nBecause $55$ is slightly closer to $49$ than to $64$ (the distance to $49$ is $6,$ and the distance to $64$ is $9$), we expect $\\sqrt{55}$ to be near the middle, but slightly less than $7.5.$ \n\nWe can test the plausible choices by squaring them to see which one results in a value closest to $55:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7.1}^{2} & =50.41 \\\\ {7.4}^{2} & =54.76 \\\\ {7.9}^{2} & =62.41\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${7.4}^{2}=54.76$ is very close to $55.$ \n\nTherefore, the best estimate for $\\sqrt{55}$ is $7.4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83d6ac5f-99e4-5420-9047-1c43a50b43a4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c6307a1-74ba-5c29-ac1d-383f075e9d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498755} {:answer/id #uuid "117d531d-98e4-5f84-8504-fea2f6f23c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498764} {:answer/id #uuid "5683a14a-1515-5481-afdc-0eeb230b0116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498926} {:answer/id #uuid "85200cd6-bfeb-5891-809c-50d32e9f5539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499030} {:answer/id #uuid "fd675d85-758d-5dfa-8620-0dc1bf8294ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5683a14a-1515-5481-afdc-0eeb230b0116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498926} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d7f175-4135-5fa7-9bc4-9394239b508a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ccb9537-59db-525f-88c4-f6dfbe10d934" :question/math-academy-id "q-555465" :question/problem "Which of the following is the best estimate for $\\sqrt[3]{85}?$" :question/worked-solution "To estimate $\\sqrt[3]{85},$ we first locate the perfect cubes that are closest to $85.$\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{3} & =64 \\\\ {5}^{3} & =125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $85$ is between $64$ and $125,$ its cube root must be between $4$ and $5.$ This eliminates any choices that do not fall in this range.\n\n![](images/9b/9b97872f5a4258a5b417f6e91bfc33733125b7b0f16299b6cddf06ce3f6cd87b.png)\n\nWe can further refine our estimate by considering distances. The number $85$ is closer to $64$ than it is to $125,$ so we expect its cube root to be closer to $4$ than to $5.$ \n\nLooking at the remaining choices—$4.1,$ $4.4,$ and $4.7$—we can test a value by cubing it to see how close the result is to $85.$ Let's test $4.4$ by cubing it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4.4}^{3} & =4.4\\cdot 4.4\\cdot 4.4 \\\\  & =19.36\\cdot 4.4 \\\\  & =85.184\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $85.184$ is very close to $85,$ we can confidently choose $4.4$ as our estimate.\n\nTherefore, the best estimate for $\\sqrt[3]{85}$ is $4.4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77b54737-402a-5ce2-acda-ea818dd80b33" :knowledge-point/title "Ordering a Set of Irrational Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81dad43c-5cfe-5746-a738-5b99783d532b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81dad43c-5cfe-5746-a738-5b99783d532b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499021} :answer-field/id #uuid "3747757d-822e-59f8-bcf9-dab681a640d1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499341} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89398cc2-da84-571a-8a77-828feeb58a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89398cc2-da84-571a-8a77-828feeb58a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499037} :answer-field/id #uuid "b30ccb71-ee09-5dc1-b0ae-907c138b3879" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3626c9d-4c67-59b5-8f71-2278f07094c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3626c9d-4c67-59b5-8f71-2278f07094c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499199} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4a1ccf9-6be7-50d1-8c39-756d5229a12a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d53369fc-0d46-5c02-b4f8-5c2f6f6fa8d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-555914" :question/problem "Consider the numbers $\\sqrt{7},$ $\\sqrt{3},$ and $\\sqrt{15}.$ Write these three numbers in order from least to greatest.\n\nThe numbers, in order from least to greatest, are:\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find two consecutive whole numbers that bracket each root. We do this by finding the perfect squares just below and just above the number under the radical.\n\n- For $\\sqrt{3}:$ \nSince $1<3<4,$ we have $\\sqrt{1}<\\sqrt{3}<\\sqrt{4}.$ This means $1<\\sqrt{3}<2.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{7}:$\nSince $4<7<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{9}.$ This means $2<\\sqrt{7}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{15}:$\nSince $9<15<16,$ we have $\\sqrt{9}<\\sqrt{15}<\\sqrt{16}.$ This means $3<\\sqrt{15}<4.$\n\nFrom these bounds, we can see that all three numbers fall into different unit intervals:\n\n$$\n1<\\sqrt{3}<2<\\sqrt{7}<3<\\sqrt{15}<4\n$$\n\nComparing these intervals, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{15}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the numbers in order from least to greatest are $\\sqrt{3},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ and $\\sqrt{15}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f6b9f6c-95f0-5c70-b22d-f60c0e9d20da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Unit Interval for a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fedb2ed-4682-5275-9d54-33d785b1c54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498801} {:answer/id #uuid "47e747eb-6df1-5d2b-a333-2e93e0573d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498895} {:answer/id #uuid "a05f684d-1b10-542e-8092-660bffcec18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499073} {:answer/id #uuid "a22e1809-2eb9-5aa4-a870-13b73e949e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499078} {:answer/id #uuid "f747a8cf-0dc0-577e-a315-77188cc397ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$19$ and $21$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a05f684d-1b10-542e-8092-660bffcec18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499073} :answer-field/id #uuid "745d57b3-89b0-568b-9cc7-e2f23619b3b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "283f891f-b99b-51a0-abad-92ec462820ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-556431" :question/problem "Between which two consecutive whole numbers does $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ lie?" :question/worked-solution "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ lies between, we can compare $20$ to nearby perfect cubes.\n\n- The closest perfect cube less than $20$ is $8,$ since ${2}^{3}=8.$\n\n- The closest perfect cube greater than $20$ is $27,$ since ${3}^{3}=27.$\n\nThis means $20$ is between $8$ and $27:$\n\n$$\n8<20<27\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{8} & <\\sqrt[3]{20}<\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\ 2 & <\\sqrt[3]{20}<3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ lies between $2$ and $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83d6ac5f-99e4-5420-9047-1c43a50b43a4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a464dee-3917-55b2-a7df-14ac5fe20416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498748} {:answer/id #uuid "0c001871-c260-5bf6-9737-544d95ebc89d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498750} {:answer/id #uuid "40fd4564-da13-5b7e-89af-9366df2bd8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498880} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6f82be-1883-5a8c-b6d5-46896e113e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499219} {:answer/id #uuid "deec2240-9409-5726-8457-08488f63b4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc6f82be-1883-5a8c-b6d5-46896e113e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499219} :answer-field/id #uuid "c16649a1-3daf-5fc0-b1e3-d24a080b601c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ec75543-e826-5114-b0c0-b95399a3601d" :question/math-academy-id "q-556439" :question/problem "Which of the following is the best estimate for $\\sqrt{73}?$" :question/worked-solution "To estimate $\\sqrt{73},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $73.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $64={8}^{2}$ and $81={9}^{2}.$\n\nSince $64<73<81,$ we know that the square root of $73$ lies between $8$ and $9.$ This tells us that $7.6$ and $36.5$ are incorrect estimates.\n\nBecause $73$ is slightly closer to $81$ than to $64$ (the distance to $64$ is $9,$ and the distance to $81$ is $8$), we expect $\\sqrt{73}$ to be near the middle of the interval.\n\nWe can test the plausible choices by squaring them to see which one results in a value closest to $73:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8.2}^{2} & =67.24 \\\\ {8.5}^{2} & =72.25 \\\\ {8.9}^{2} & =79.21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${8.5}^{2}=72.25$ is very close to $73.$\n\nTherefore, the best estimate for $\\sqrt{73}$ is $8.5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83d6ac5f-99e4-5420-9047-1c43a50b43a4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12a8fa1f-db3c-549c-8ff7-0928127d5ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498768} {:answer/id #uuid "2b51bb34-2903-5516-a067-83b068bc6daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498823} {:answer/id #uuid "5c00f9a5-d81f-5aeb-9c70-b3d58c57992c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498940} {:answer/id #uuid "80a91325-3ae9-57ec-822f-83c4f37e0cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499019} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9ab33f-37a9-5078-bdf6-fca039d691a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12a8fa1f-db3c-549c-8ff7-0928127d5ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498768} :answer-field/id #uuid "516f3cb0-cb44-5f92-86e6-bf5365f546b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f1f35ac-b2a3-5225-badd-5521e9e9745c" :question/math-academy-id "q-556440" :question/problem "Which of the following is the best estimate for $\\sqrt{29}?$" :question/worked-solution "To estimate $\\sqrt{29},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $29.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $25={5}^{2}$ and $36={6}^{2}.$\n\nSince $25<29<36,$ we know that the square root of $29$ lies between $5$ and $6.$ This tells us that $4.9$ and $14.5$ are incorrect estimates.\n\nBecause $29$ is much closer to $25$ than to $36$ (the distance to $25$ is $4,$ and the distance to $36$ is $7$), we expect $\\sqrt{29}$ to be near the lower end of the interval.\n\nWe can test the plausible choices by squaring them to see which one results in a value closest to $29:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5.4}^{2} & =29.16 \\\\ {5.7}^{2} & =32.49 \\\\ {5.9}^{2} & =34.81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${5.4}^{2}=29.16$ is very close to $29.$\n\nTherefore, the best estimate for $\\sqrt{29}$ is $5.4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77b54737-402a-5ce2-acda-ea818dd80b33" :knowledge-point/title "Ordering a Set of Irrational Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "977a7ca4-4933-58a4-a9dc-e7c4af72a5fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{220}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499055} {:answer/id #uuid "9f84279f-7de6-5ecc-94ab-b153c6df0574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499069} {:answer/id #uuid "abce5e5e-e001-57eb-9c66-566dc8020b46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{250}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499108} {:answer/id #uuid "c435c8d4-2ebb-5e38-b381-174697adb4eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499161} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f9926a-82d7-5eee-85a6-919f9d530f1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abce5e5e-e001-57eb-9c66-566dc8020b46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{250}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499108} :answer-field/id #uuid "3411bd67-62ed-5109-9374-7e6e92efa409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f4f8da7-ea6d-5fee-9cf3-3eb27646ae68" :question/math-academy-id "q-556442" :question/problem "Which of the following irrational numbers is the greatest?" :question/worked-solution "To identify the greatest irrational number, we can find the consecutive whole numbers between which each root lies.\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{250}$ lies between $6$ and $7,$ since ${6}^{3}=216$ and ${7}^{3}=343.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{220}$ lies between $6$ and $7,$ since ${6}^{3}=216$ and ${7}^{3}=343.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{35}$ lies between $5$ and $6,$ since ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36.$\n\n- $\\sqrt{28}$ lies between $5$ and $6,$ since ${5}^{2}=25$ and ${6}^{2}=36.$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{100}$ lies between $4$ and $5,$ since ${4}^{3}=64$ and ${5}^{3}=125.$\n\nComparing these bounds, the greatest number must be between $6$ and $7.$ So, it is either $\\sqrt[3]{250}$ or $\\sqrt[3]{220}.$\n\nSince $250>220,$ we know that $\\sqrt[3]{250}>\\sqrt[3]{220}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{250}$ is the greatest number among the given options."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e25ce9f-c93f-5dc0-9e81-2093663c6202" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decimal Interval for a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09ab94cd-e53f-5030-9794-55a45a2d199a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9.1$ and $9.2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498747} {:answer/id #uuid "22ffcbe1-1311-5383-ae08-7dc9f5a89559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9.4$ and $9.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498809} {:answer/id #uuid "2d21cd52-8839-5972-8ad9-4b183e6aa799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9.2$ and $9.3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498829} {:answer/id #uuid "9048679d-6b21-5f81-8bf5-0219c0264b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9.3$ and $9.4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499044} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ed41e3-39ce-5d9a-99d7-78fcd3a62e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9.0$ and $9.1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d21cd52-8839-5972-8ad9-4b183e6aa799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9.2$ and $9.3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498829} :answer-field/id #uuid "d68cf0b2-0983-5dca-947f-dad9ec7dbabf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eee5e7a8-3d1e-565b-891d-6f10ecae6e7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-556444" :question/problem "Between which two consecutive tenths does $\\sqrt{85}$ lie?" :question/worked-solution "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt{85}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose squares bracket $85$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt{85}$ lies.\nWe know that ${9}^{2}=81$ and ${10}^{2}=100$.\nSince $81<85<100$, we know that $9<\\sqrt{85}<10$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $9$ and $10$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9.1}^{2} & =82.81 \\\\ {9.2}^{2} & =84.64 \\\\ {9.3}^{2} & =86.49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $84.64<85<86.49$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{9.2}^{2}<85<{9.3}^{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n9.2<\\sqrt{85}<9.3\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{85}$ lies between $9.2$ and $9.3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e25ce9f-c93f-5dc0-9e81-2093663c6202" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decimal Interval for a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "801c3de2-46b2-563c-ad74-0c2d98a2cfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "801c3de2-46b2-563c-ad74-0c2d98a2cfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499018} :answer-field/id #uuid "643419a5-aa7e-539b-9065-e765e48f0a7a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499362} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e10eadb7-d0b9-516d-a436-28a7ccc472d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e10eadb7-d0b9-516d-a436-28a7ccc472d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499233} :answer-field/id #uuid "648cf6d9-b9fa-597f-93e1-1e473afc1140" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a16d859-8fe1-5bbc-a36b-6b57bd9a46c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-556446" :question/problem "The value of $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ lies between two consecutive tenths $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand\n$b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $50$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ lies.\nWe know that ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${4}^{3}=64$.\nSince $27<50<64$, we know that $3<\\sqrt[3]{50}<4$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $3$ and $4$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3.5}^{3} & =42.875 \\\\ {3.6}^{3} & =46.656 \\\\ {3.7}^{3} & =50.653 \\\\ {3.8}^{3} & =54.872\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $46.656<50<50.653$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{3.6}^{3}<50<{3.7}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n3.6<\\sqrt[3]{50}<3.7\n$$\n\nSo, $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ lies between $3.6$ and $3.7$. Since we are given that $a<b$, we have $a=3.6$ and $b=3.7$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e25ce9f-c93f-5dc0-9e81-2093663c6202" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decimal Interval for a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c95abe2c-4f68-5d3c-854b-bebbf38a7f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c95abe2c-4f68-5d3c-854b-bebbf38a7f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499170} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec4923e3-08ed-59f1-a210-49edf0817c27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499421} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "499ef7be-c65b-5209-a84c-a4485798a68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "499ef7be-c65b-5209-a84c-a4485798a68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498900} :answer-field/id #uuid "faa793b1-4a54-5d15-9029-117fb1a34d0c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4cff9fda-568d-5f3b-ab6b-f2c93ac02430" :question/math-academy-id "q-556447" :question/problem "The value of $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ lies between two consecutive tenths $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand\n$b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $10$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ lies.\nWe know that ${2}^{3}=8$ and ${3}^{3}=27$.\nSince $8<10<27$, we know that $2<\\sqrt[3]{10}<3$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $2$ and $3$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2.0}^{3} & =8 \\\\ {2.1}^{3} & =9.261 \\\\ {2.2}^{3} & =10.648 \\\\ {2.3}^{3} & =12.167\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $9.261<10<10.648$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{2.1}^{3}<10<{2.2}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n2.1<\\sqrt[3]{10}<2.2\n$$\n\nSo, $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ lies between $2.1$ and $2.2$. Since we are given that $a<b$, we have $a=2.1$ and $b=2.2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e25ce9f-c93f-5dc0-9e81-2093663c6202" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decimal Interval for a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5f6962-b775-55e8-9685-83c9a8b7ed56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.7$ and $3.8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498795} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4c227c-f3fd-5e20-b4e7-30a7351a37fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.4$ and $5.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498871} {:answer/id #uuid "8934d595-91f0-55e0-b2e6-ed53be38d094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.3$ and $5.4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499036} {:answer/id #uuid "a5838ac1-4595-5be0-885a-21b66891f9d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.5$ and $5.6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499090} {:answer/id #uuid "f94d9e65-45f5-5149-92ec-9f358b105729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.2$ and $5.3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8934d595-91f0-55e0-b2e6-ed53be38d094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.3$ and $5.4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499036} :answer-field/id #uuid "36bfb1eb-f137-5f6f-9630-d9ca368026c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbc69846-f4f2-5c08-841b-d6a9c38b8533" :question/math-academy-id "q-556449" :question/problem "Between which two consecutive tenths does $\\sqrt[3]{150}$ lie?" :question/worked-solution "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{150}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $150$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{150}$ lies.\nWe know that ${5}^{3}=125$ and ${6}^{3}=216$.\nSince $125<150<216$, we know that $5<\\sqrt[3]{150}<6$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $5$ and $6$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5.2}^{3} & =140.608 \\\\ {5.3}^{3} & =148.877 \\\\ {5.4}^{3} & =157.464\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $148.877<150<157.464$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{5.3}^{3}<150<{5.4}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n5.3<\\sqrt[3]{150}<5.4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{150}$ lies between $5.3$ and $5.4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77b54737-402a-5ce2-acda-ea818dd80b33" :knowledge-point/title "Ordering a Set of Irrational Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cc29c4e-ec58-5b08-9e0a-929c54b9bbef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cc29c4e-ec58-5b08-9e0a-929c54b9bbef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498791} :answer-field/id #uuid "2760b992-09fe-54cf-af2f-15bbcbde2bdb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499329} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95b5a366-a285-587e-a7ce-81b79d2706de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95b5a366-a285-587e-a7ce-81b79d2706de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499054} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9e423ea-407f-5b27-8c11-fd5320a77861" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499393} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "903d0b1b-7527-5803-a385-024d69b24c6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "903d0b1b-7527-5803-a385-024d69b24c6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499043} :answer-field/id #uuid "d33d2751-7e75-5dbd-bca0-6496988af9d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c62dfe4-3d3e-58f1-bc84-569e3d239868" :question/math-academy-id "q-556452" :question/problem "Consider the numbers $\\sqrt{20},$ $\\sqrt{5},$ and $\\sqrt{11}.$ Write these three numbers in order from least to greatest.\n\nThe numbers, in order from least to greatest, are:\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find two consecutive whole numbers that bracket each root. We do this by finding the perfect squares just below and just above the number under the radical.\n\n- For $\\sqrt{5}:$ \nSince $4<5<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{5}<\\sqrt{9}.$ This means $2<\\sqrt{5}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{11}:$\nSince $9<11<16,$ we have $\\sqrt{9}<\\sqrt{11}<\\sqrt{16}.$ This means $3<\\sqrt{11}<4.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{20}:$\nSince $16<20<25,$ we have $\\sqrt{16}<\\sqrt{20}<\\sqrt{25}.$ This means $4<\\sqrt{20}<5.$\n\nFrom these bounds, we can see that all three numbers fall into different unit intervals:\n\n$$\n2<\\sqrt{5}<3<\\sqrt{11}<4<\\sqrt{20}<5\n$$\n\nComparing these intervals, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}<\\sqrt{11}<\\sqrt{20}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the numbers in order from least to greatest are $\\sqrt{5},$ $\\sqrt{11},$ and $\\sqrt{20}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77b54737-402a-5ce2-acda-ea818dd80b33" :knowledge-point/title "Ordering a Set of Irrational Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3df0edd1-81de-5dc7-bf9d-070d5092683d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3df0edd1-81de-5dc7-bf9d-070d5092683d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498873} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bf68efe-ba6a-5c86-a64b-eac1b2a52670" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499316} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd163a8e-84d9-57bb-9aa8-d32182147119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd163a8e-84d9-57bb-9aa8-d32182147119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499151} :answer-field/id #uuid "296ffddb-57f3-5e82-8004-569d6ecc2c8b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499332} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "386d47cd-f86e-52af-ad14-6e2e9a99775a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "386d47cd-f86e-52af-ad14-6e2e9a99775a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498853} :answer-field/id #uuid "c45d8af0-cf84-5a43-9ff7-0caf13a20bd2" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eabdbec5-6ee0-5977-9aec-0268ef8c060f" :question/math-academy-id "q-556457" :question/problem "Consider the numbers $\\sqrt[3]{15},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ and $\\sqrt[3]{50}.$ Write these three numbers in increasing order.\n\nThe three numbers in increasing order are\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find two consecutive whole numbers that bracket each root. We do this by finding the perfect powers just below and just above the number under the radical.\n\n- For $\\sqrt[3]{15}:$\nSince $8<15<27,$ we have $\\sqrt[3]{8}<\\sqrt[3]{15}<\\sqrt[3]{27}.$ This means $2<\\sqrt[3]{15}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{7}:$\nSince $4<7<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt{9}.$ This means $2<\\sqrt{7}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt[3]{50}:$\nSince $27<50<64,$ we have $\\sqrt[3]{27}<\\sqrt[3]{50}<\\sqrt[3]{64}.$ This means $3<\\sqrt[3]{50}<4.$\n\nFrom these bounds, we can see that $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ is the greatest of the three numbers because it is greater than $3,$ while the other two are less than $3.$ \n\nHowever, both $\\sqrt[3]{15}$ and $\\sqrt{7}$ are between $2$ and $3.$ To compare them, we need to test some decimal values. We can square or cube decimals to find closer approximations:\n\n- Testing tenths for $\\sqrt[3]{15}:$ \nSince ${2.4}^{3}=13.824$ and ${2.5}^{3}=15.625,$ we know that $2.4<\\sqrt[3]{15}<2.5.$\n\n- Testing tenths for $\\sqrt{7}:$\nSince ${2.6}^{2}=6.76$ and ${2.7}^{2}=7.29,$ we know that $2.6<\\sqrt{7}<2.7.$\n\nComparing these tighter bounds, we see that $\\sqrt[3]{15}<2.5<2.6<\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nCombining all our comparisons, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{15}<\\sqrt{7}<\\sqrt[3]{50}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three numbers in increasing order are $\\sqrt[3]{15},$ $\\sqrt{7},$ and $\\sqrt[3]{50}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f6b9f6c-95f0-5c70-b22d-f60c0e9d20da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Unit Interval for a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cdd0b59-ed03-5b0b-bacc-96c33578d2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cdd0b59-ed03-5b0b-bacc-96c33578d2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499009} :answer-field/id #uuid "0758f499-2222-5ddd-96de-36db1303c8da" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499312} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7563557c-409e-5dcc-9ad7-abc29a1f61a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7563557c-409e-5dcc-9ad7-abc29a1f61a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498992} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a203bbe-0b10-5512-8854-06888a0f874a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cefb6c0d-04aa-5eeb-98b9-43235f7ba892" :question/math-academy-id "q-556458" :question/problem "The value of $\\sqrt[3]{30}$ lies between two consecutive whole numbers $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt[3]{30}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect cubes that are just below and just above $30.$\n\nThe perfect cubes closest to $30$ are $27$ and $64.$ We have\n\n$$\n27<30<64.\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{27} & <\\sqrt[3]{30}<\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ 3 & <\\sqrt[3]{30}<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/3e/3efc92b2664e8fade27edc67f977d78b317e73343d3201ac38c1fad807cdb01a.png)\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt[3]{30}$ lies between the consecutive whole numbers $3$ and $4.$ \n\nTherefore, since $a<b,$ we have $a=3$ and $b=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f6b9f6c-95f0-5c70-b22d-f60c0e9d20da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Unit Interval for a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f2df599-3b22-56a9-8667-5c6ddefde3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f2df599-3b22-56a9-8667-5c6ddefde3bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498915} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c3ce8d1-67ff-5cff-a091-8260f1794f3c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499335} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6ad1fc7-a7f5-503f-a014-36307209a247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6ad1fc7-a7f5-503f-a014-36307209a247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499136} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bbdce47-285c-55a9-b31d-8f13130a183b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b525db8-241b-5276-b6b9-4b8689047d2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-556462" :question/problem "The value of $\\sqrt{110}$ lies between two consecutive whole numbers $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt{110}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect squares that are just below and just above $110.$\n\nThe perfect squares closest to $110$ are $100$ and $121.$ We have\n\n$$\n100<110<121.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{100} & <\\sqrt{110}<\\sqrt{121} \\\\ 10 & <\\sqrt{110}<11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/9b/9bef06cb0000173c30147f4f9f633f3adec207b688adb005bcf0fff1aa8d7b13.png)\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt{110}$ lies between the consecutive whole numbers $10$ and $11.$ \n\nTherefore, since $a<b,$ we have $a=10$ and $b=11.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83d6ac5f-99e4-5420-9047-1c43a50b43a4" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Root of an Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c846da4f-defa-517a-887f-4bb071b66588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c846da4f-defa-517a-887f-4bb071b66588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499168} :answer-field/id #uuid "28e89b58-7411-51b4-b86e-1c63a2770f1a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b9de470-595d-5301-8412-daba962c6a6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-556486" :question/problem "Estimate the value of $\\sqrt[3]{20},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{20}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To estimate $\\sqrt[3]{20},$ we first find the two perfect cubes closest to $20.$\n\nThe closest perfect cubes are $8={2}^{3}$ and $27={3}^{3}.$\n\nSince $8<20<27,$ we know that the cube root of $20$ lies between $2$ and $3.$\n\n![](images/c4/c4eb7ee1951d0d402df1553d46bc6e8ce1fdb421adc40949b9e79964484892c6.png)\n\nBecause $20$ is closer to $27$ than to $8,$ we expect $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ to be close to $3.$ \n\nLet's test a decimal near $3,$ such as $2.7,$ by cubing it:\n\n$$\n{2.7}^{3}=19.683\n$$\n\nSince $19.683$ is slightly less than $20,$ we know that $2.7<\\sqrt[3]{20}<2.8.$\n\nNext, we determine whether $20$ is closer to ${2.7}^{3}$ or ${2.8}^{3}:$\n\n- The distance from $20$ to ${2.7}^{3}$ is $20-19.683=0.317.$\n\n- The distance from $20$ to ${2.8}^{3}$ is $21.952-20=1.952.$\n\nSince $20$ is much closer to $19.683,$ $\\sqrt[3]{20}$ is closer to $2.7.$\n\nTherefore, rounded to $1$ decimal place, $\\sqrt[3]{20}≈2.7.$"}]}
